The Nethack license says (Section 2(c)):
> You may charge a distribution fee for the physical act of
> transferring a copy

But also says (Section 2(b)):
> cause the whole of any work that you distribute or publish, that in
> whole or in part contains or is a derivative of NetHack or any part
> thereof, to be licensed at no charge to all third parties on terms
> identical to those contained in this License Agreement

In other words, you can charge people for transferring a copy to them,
but you can't stop them from giving it away for free to other people.

> He makes the same claim for the GPL itself.

The GPL (version 2) has a similar clause (Section 1):
> You may charge a fee for the physical act of transferring a copy, and
> you may at your option offer warranty protection in exchange for a
> fee.

And version 3 makes it even more explicit (Section 4):
> You may charge any price or no price for each copy that you convey,
> and you may offer support or warranty protection for a fee.

Further reading on the subject from the GNU Project:
http://www.gnu.org/philosophy/selling.html

